Ukraine says more than 100 civilians  were evacuated from the besieged as of  style steel plant in mariu pole on  Sunday the plant is the last part of the  city not occupied by Russian forces  hundreds more people are still trapped  there along with around 2 000 Ukrainian  soldiers evacuations were carried in  coordination with United Nations and Red  Cross after talks to open a humanitarian  corridor  rescued from the rubble the civilian  survivors of the as of style steel plant  climbed from the darkness of bunkers  under the cover of a two-day ceasefire  video released by The as of regiment  show the final steps of an almost  unbearable weight for help  we've been here for two months  her baby turns six months old a day  earlier a young life spent under siege  these children tell how they almost ran  out of food  the children wanted to eat all the time  you know adults can endure the  evacuations were the result of tireless  diplomacy praised by Ukrainian president  of Vladimir zielinsky  after many weeks of negotiations after  many attempts different meetings people  calls countries proposals finally there  was not a single day when we did not try  to find a solution that would save our  people  Russia's military also released its  video of people being transported from  as of style and taken to camps it says  those wishing to be evacuated to  ukrainian-held territory will be passed  over to the United Nations and Red Cross  the survivor's best stories of existing  in squalid conditions in oxygen-deprived  bunkers  I can't believe it  two months of darkness when we were in  the bus I told my husband we won't have  to use a flashlight to go to the toilet  in a bag and Bin we didn't see any  sunlight we were scared we foreign  and Red Cross hope to help more  civilians out of mariupole hundreds  still remain in as of style  waiting for a similar Escape  is standing by for us in key fenney it's  good to see you around 100 civilians  were able to leave that as of style on  Sunday what are the plans to get the  rest of them out  definitely there's a call on making sure  more and more people get out in fact  behind me you see this Banner that says  in Ukrainian Mario Paul which translates  in English to d block Mario Paul now  that city is unlikely to be liberated at  this point because it's controlled by  Russia for the most part except for that  steel plant where hundreds of people  remain trapped but those 100 civilians  that could leave the steel plant  yesterday and really we are speaking  about children who see sunlight again  for the first time in weeks who actually  get some fresh water and and basic  things more people would like to enjoy  at this a freedom again but will they  that's the question now the city council  of maripole said that in fact a bigger  evacuation is due to start this morning  but it was already delayed from  yesterday also reports that shelling  resumed after this first group of people  left the steel plant as a result really  the question is will this evacuation  continue Mary Paul how many more people  are going to leave certainly the people  who are inside would like to get out not  only civilians but also wounded soldiers  and let's not forget unfortunately  according to information inside they're  also that body is that are decomposing  it's really harrowing just imagine that  alone experience what people are going  through inside the steel plant as we are  speaking we mentioned them there those  soldiers what's the plan to evacuate  them are they part of this plan funny  the deputy commander of the as of  regiment made clear that they want  everyone out there both civilians and  soldiers but at the same time this  Deputy Commander ultimately had they not  been inside to defend the steel plant  and then the entire Frontline actually  of Russia would be even further up  towards the north so he's basically also  justifying in a way already that it was  very important and it is important to  defend the steel plant to make sure to  prevent that Russia advances even  further which is obviously stands in in  in the line of what the Russian plan is  to advance especially in the donbass  region but also in the southern part of  Ukraine whether more people are going to  get out we are not only speaking about 1  000 civilians inside that's the estimate  but about 2 000 soldiers who are also  inside or underneath this steel plant  you have to imagine this is like a  system of various bunkers partly child  partly with mines it's very difficult to  get people out out and the next day I'll  just have to see and prove whether  that's going to be possible and a  ceasefire can be maintained to help  people out from the steel plant in Mario  pool funny thanks very much for that  update DW correspondent Fanning fascia  and keys  well for more I'm joined by Frank  ledwich he's a senior lecturer in  strategy and law at the University of  Portsmouth and a former military  intelligence officer Frank thanks very  much to speaking for speaking to us this  morning so some hundred civilians have  been evacuated that does give some hope  tell us why haven't Russian troops been  able to take that steel plant do you  think  as first of all it's extremely difficult  to take any built up area you need the  rules say essentially you need a five to  one preponderance of forces in built up  areas and something like the steel plant  is absolutely vicious again for  attackers this reminds historians owning  almost anyone who knows any about  military history very much of the  tractor Factory at Stalingrad which the  Russians held on to it's iconic in  Russian military history they held on to  it for three months and it's it's very  possible to happen here but also Russian  difficulties compounded by the fact that  during the Cold War the as of style  complex was was treated as one of the  sort of cores of the cause as in the  centers of the nuclear defense system so  it's filled with underground bunkers  it'd be really difficult to clear those  out so it's a really difficult nut to  crack for Russians elsewhere the Russian  troops have made minor advances  particularly in the Don bass but on the  other hand ukrainians Ukrainian soldiers  have managed to push back in some areas  particularly taking back some key  villages in the khakive region and these  front lines seem to be barely moving are  we in the midst of a war of attrition  very much so this is a grinding more of  artillery of small movements of maneuver  Warfare and the ukrainians are far  better than that than the Russians at  that than the Russians interestingly in  the north there you mentioned kharkiv  the the problem for the Russians there  is the Ukraine is getting quite close to  being able to Shell some of the Russian  supply routes to the South so some  analysts are looking at this and saying  well this this may result in the  Russians actually withdrawing forces  from the donbass area north to try and  defend that but we'll see see that  what's happening in the South have is  very interesting reports indicate that  yesterday the Russian chief of the  general staff General grassimov has  almost caught in an artillery strike  which killed dozens of officers and he  was there evidently to see what's  holding all this up and of course what's  holding it up is the endemic ramshackle  nature of the Russian army it's simply  not up to Modern standards but that's a  set of issues they're making very slow  progress Rebecca all right Frank  unfortunately we're gonna have to leave  it there Frank ledwich for a former  military intelligence offer thanks very  much for your time  thanks Rebecca  well more than 5 million ukrainians have  now fled their country since the War  Began according to the United Nations  the vast majority of them are women and  children in the western Ukrainian city  of Lev The Exodus means some schools are  virtually empty one of them is using its  empty classrooms to provide shelter to  refugees from other parts of the country  since the war started Ivan lazensko's  Ukrainian German School in neviv has  drastically changed although we meet him  on a weekday there is no child inside  of course the school has changed a lot  because 43 percent of the children now  live abroad  they still actively take part in remote  learning classes  the school's premises didn't remain  empty for long with the help of parents  and volunteers Yvonne decided to host  internally displaced people from the  regions worst affected by the war so far  almost 200 people have stayed here  mostly men as they are not allowed to  leave the country  Vladimir arrived to the school at the  end of March he fled from demudif near  Kiev carrying only a few belongings and  this paper was him where he wrote down  that after a stroke he couldn't talk  very fast and needed immediate  evacuation  we just walked there was a 50-50 chance  to end up dead or alive we walked  through  we walked past the dam  on the road to the bridge which was  blown up by our army  I passed the first Russian checkpoint  without problems  but on the second I was stopped and they  asked me what do you have here  when I said my cell phone it was taken  away  through us  because  Vladimir sleeps in what used to be the  school's main hall now transformed into  a dormitory  there we also meet Alexandra who hopes  to join the Ukraine and Armed Forces  I came alone my family stayed near  Harkins we live in the northern part  closer to Russia and it's been bombed  every day since the beginning of the war  nearly everyone in the neighborhood left  town  I still have to pass a medical check to  join the Army and after that I don't  know I don't have a military specialty  so I'll probably have to train for a few  months  in what used to be classrooms we meet  mikhailo and his son-in-law Dennis from  butcher their wives now live in Germany  they have lost all of their possessions  and only brought Mickey the family dog  on the evening of February 28th Russian  soldiers knocked on Denny's door  Russian troops fought to be removed me  from a flat and came with rifles they  searched me broke my mobile phones and  stole my laptops they brought me to the  nearby church and told me not to come  outside until sunrise they set up their  military position and their artillery  there's only me and two other men in the  morning we left and settled here  [Music]  in his school Ivan provides some relief  to fellow ukrainians who have lost  everything but those displaced people  and especially men are left in limbo  here in Nevada not knowing if and when  they will be able to return home  like in Ukraine war has separated  families across the country some have  fled war-torn areas leaving others  behind to fight DW has been speaking to  a woman in Western Ukraine who received  a very special video message from her  boyfriend fighting on the front line  I can rely on you in difficult times I  love you very much I don't have a  wedding ring for you right now but I do  have a ring on this grenade I would love  you to be my wife please marry me  was hoping for a sign of life from her  boyfriend when she looked at her phone  three weeks ago then she saw the video  that would change her life forever  I wanted to say yes instantly I tried to  text him or call but I couldn't get  through then he finally called back and  I said how could I refuse you especially  with a grenade in your hand  after the War Began Anastasia fled to  the city of leviv her boyfriend went to  fight in the donbass region one of the  most dangerous War zones sometimes he  sends her little videos and of the photo  he always carries in his first aid bag  but Anastasia hasn't heard from him for  days  there are moments when I can't take it  anymore and I start crying I try to make  sure no one sees me I don't want anyone  to feel sorry for me  oh I know is if something happens to him  oh God  according to martial law ukrainians can  even get married remotely but in  Anastasia's case that's not an option  he is in a very difficult place  there's no internet no pens not even  paper  he can't even wash himself properly  Anastasia says at some point the war  will end then they will get married  properly and she will no longer have to  walk alone

